---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="Accessibility · Bundu standards" description="How standards.bundu.org and vocab.bundu.org work for everyone, with a keyboard, a screen reader, zoom or colour-blind vision, and how to report a barrier.">
  <h1>Accessibility</h1>
  <p>
    The standards register and the Bundu vocabulary are public infrastructure, so everyone has to be able to use
    them. Both sites are built to the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 at level AA, and to the Bundu
    floor, which goes further: body text at 7:1 contrast, text no smaller than 14px, and controls large enough to
    hit.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keyboard">Using a keyboard</h2>
  <p>Every page works with a keyboard alone. Nothing needs a mouse, and nothing traps the focus.</p>
  <table>
    <caption>Keys</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><kbd>Tab</kbd></td><td>Moves to the next link or control. The first stop on every page is <em>Skip to main content</em>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Tab</kbd></td><td>Moves back to the previous link or control.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><kbd>Enter</kbd></td><td>Follows the focused link.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><kbd>Space</kbd> or <kbd>Page Down</kbd></td><td>Scrolls the page down; <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Space</kbd> or <kbd>Page Up</kbd> scrolls up.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arrow keys</td><td>Scroll an example block sideways once it has the focus.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The focused item always shows a visible outline in copper. On the register, <em>On this page</em> jumps straight
    to each group of standards.
  </p>

  <h2 id="screen-readers">Using a screen reader</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Each page has one main region, a labelled main navigation and a footer, so landmark navigation works.</li>
    <li>Headings are in order: one level-one heading per page, then a level-two heading for each section.</li>
    <li>The page you are on is announced as the current page in the navigation.</li>
    <li>A standard's status is read as “Status: adopted” or “Status: proposed”, not as a colour.</li>
    <li>Links say where they go, including the ones whose visible text is short, such as “JSON” and “Source”.</li>
    <li>Images that only decorate, such as the Bundu mark beside the site name, are hidden from screen readers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="vision">Zoom, contrast and colour</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Text can be zoomed to 400% and the page reflows to one column with no sideways scrolling.</li>
    <li>Light and dark themes follow your device setting. Both meet the contrast floor.</li>
    <li>Colour never carries meaning on its own: every status and every colour-scale band also has a text label.</li>
    <li>
      The weather colour scales publish, for each band, where its label goes and which surfaces it needs an outline
      on, and the scales we designed stay distinguishable for people with red, green or blue colour blindness.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="data">Data without the pages</h2>
  <p>
    Everything on these pages is also available as data: the register as <a href="/register/index.json">JSON</a> and
    each vocabulary term as JSON-LD. Assistive tools and scripts can read it directly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checked">How this is checked</h2>
  <p>
    Every change runs the site's tests, which fail if the skip link, the labelled navigation, the main region or a
    text label goes missing. The pages are also audited with axe-core against WCAG 2.2 AA in the light and dark
    themes; the last audit, on 15 November 2026, found no violations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">Report a barrier</h2>
  <p>
    If something on these sites does not work for you, tell us. Open an issue at
    <a href="https://github.com/bundu-labs/bundu-standards/issues/new?title=Accessibility%3A%20">github.com/bundu-labs/bundu-standards</a>,
    saying which page, what you were trying to do and what you use (for example a screen reader or zoom). A barrier is
    treated as a bug, not a feature request.
  </p>
</Base>
